{"type":"message","id":"ee00b4ee","parentId":"f066eb39","timestamp":"2026-03-26T01:25:20.278Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I can't run this script directly because I don't have the actual App ID, App Secret, and User Token environment variables. Let me try a different approach.\n\nActually, let me look at this from a completely different angle. The issue is:\n1. `feishu_drive_file.upload` returns without `file_token`\n2. Every API call needs re-auth\n\nWhat if the problem is simpler? When the plugin makes the API call, it's using `as: 'user'` which means it's using the user's OAuth token. But maybe the response from `uploadAll` is being processed differently.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the plugin code once more:\n\n\nThe uploadAll API call returns a response, and the plugin expects `res.data?.file_token` to contain the uploaded file's token. But I'm getting back `{ file_name: \"demo_weather.png\", size: 52567 }` instead, which means `res.data` exists but doesn't have the `file_token` field. This could indicate the upload actually failed silently, or the response structure is different than what the plugin expects.\n\nLooking at the Feishu API documentation, `upload_all` returns `file_token` as a string directly in the data object, not nested deeper. So either the SDK wraps the response in an extra layer, or there's something else going on with how the response is being parsed.\n\nI'm thinking the most direct solution is to write a small Python script using the requests library to call the Feishu API directly, cutting out any potential SDK issues. I'd need to extract the user access token from the environment or config files to authenticate the request properly.","thinkingSignature":"51c6c337bc608264e71c870dbc89757cde68de147cc1e7005e00f7ee1d7345a7"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_pcnfep6etvvp_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"find /Users/edy/.openclaw -name \"*.json\" -path \"*/lark/*\" -o -name \"*.js