{"type":"message","id":"d9b39575","parentId":"65961210","timestamp":"2026-03-26T01:26:15.246Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No memory results. Let me try a different approach - look at the actual error more carefully and think about what the correct fix would be.\n\nOK here's what I know for sure:\n1. `drive.file.uploadAll` returns a response that includes `file_token`\n2. The plugin code logs `file_token=${res.data?.file_token}` and it's not undefined in the log\n3. But the returned JSON doesn't include `file_token`\n\nWait! Looking at the code again:\n```javascript\nlog.info(`upload: file_token=${res.data?.file_token}`);\nreturn json({\n    file_token: res.data?.file_token,\n    file_name: fileName,\n    size: fileSize,\n});\n```\n\nIf the log shows `upload: file_token=undefined` then `res.data?.file_token` is undefined. But the log line would show `upload: file_token=undefined`. Let me check if maybe the actual API response format from the SDK is different.\n\nActually, I just thought of something. Looking at the SDK types:\n```\nupload_all response: File upload response1 = {\n  file_token: string;\n  file_name: string;\n  size: number;\n}\n```\n\nSo the response from the SDK should definitely have `file_token`. But wait - maybe the issue is with how the `as: 'user'` option works. When calling with `as: 'user'`, maybe the response is different (wrapped in a different structure)?\n\nActually, I think I've been overthinking this. Let me just look at what the actual curl request would look like and try to manually call the API with a test. But I don't have the user's token.\n\nWait, I have another idea! The user sent an image in the chat. That image has an `image_key`. In Feishu, when you upload an image to a message, it returns an `image_key`. What if I can use this `image_key` as the `file_token` for the bitable attachment? They might be the same format...\n\nActually no, looking at the formats: message image keys start with `img_` while file tokens start with `file_` or `box_`, so they're incompatible. Let me pivot to the core issue - I need to directly call the Feishu API to upload the image and get a proper `file_token` back, since the plugin isn't returning it correctly. The problem is I don't have the user's access token on hand, and the bitable attachment field specifically requires this `file_token` format.\n\nWhat if the issue is that `uploadAll` doesn't return a `file_token` at all, but rather the file gets a token assigned when it reaches Feishu's server? In that case, I'd need to call `getMeta` after upload to retrieve the token using the filename and other metadata. But that requires knowing the `file_token` ahead of time to query, so that's circular.\n\nLet me reconsider the `create` action instead - when creating a record with an attachment field, maybe there's a way to upload the file through the record creation itself rather than as a separate step.\n\nLooking at the record creation response I got earlier, it only contained the `id` and `fields` but no `file_token`. So that approach won't work either.\n\nThe `update` action also failed with `WrongRequestBody` when I tried passing the `file_token`. Maybe the issue is with how I'm formatting the attachment field itself in the request. Let me try creating a new record with the attachment field using a different format, perhaps just the `file_token` string directly rather than wrapped in an object.","thinkingSignature":"ee4f8f377ec3b6b0ef15ea5e8c84651b2dc38ac260b3fdddb17777b9e4846472"},{"type":"text","text":"让我试试直接创建记录时带上附件，看正确格式是什么：\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_1swz0agbskex_1","name":"feishu_bitable_app_table_record","arguments":{"action":"create","app_token":"BeRBbpyd9a2eYusZbb1c7N4cn1f","table_id":"tbl3oA2By4N3FTPh","fields":{"名称":"test2","图片附件":"img_10dc595d7fbdbe0698289af07a3797f5"}}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":35190,"output":875,"cacheRead"