{"type":"message","id":"9c8cec8b","parentId":"36815f5d","timestamp":"2026-03-26T01:28:17.140Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Hmm, the dist directory doesn't seem to have the compiled version. Let me look in the node_modules for the api_error definition.Actually, let me take a step back. I've been trying to debug this upload issue for a while. The core finding is:\n\n1. The `upload_all` API call succeeds (returns `{ file_name: \"demo_weather.png\", size: 52567 }`)\n2. But `file_token` is missing from the response\n3. The SDK returns `res.data` from the HTTP response\n4. If Feishu API returns `{ code: 0, data: { file_token: \"boxcmxxx\", file_name: \"...\", size: \"...\" } }`, then `res.data` would be `{ file_token: \"boxcmxxx\", ... }`\n\nSo either:\na) The Feishu API is returning the data in a different structure\nb) The access token used doesn't have permission to get the file_token\n\n\nc) There's some response transformation happening\n\nWait - I just realized something important. Looking at the code:\n```javascript\nconst res = await client.invoke('feishu_drive_file.upload', (sdk, opts) => sdk.drive.file.uploadAll({...}, opts), { as: 'user' });\n```\n\nThe `as: 'user'` option tells the SDK to use the user's OAuth token. When using `as: 'user'`, maybe the response structure is different...\n\nLet me check the invoke function in the tool-client to understand what `as: 'user'` does to the response.\n\nActually, let me try a completely different approach. Instead of debugging why `file_token` isn't in the response, what if I just look at what the user is trying to accomplish? They want to add an attachment to a bitable record. What if there's a way to do this WITHOUT using `drive.file.uploadAll` first? For example, using the bitable API's record creation with the attachment directly.\n\nLet me look up how to create a bitable record with an attachment. According to Feishu docs, you pass the file_token of an uploaded file. So you MUST upload the file first.\n\nOK here's another idea - what if I can get the file_token from a DIFFERENT API? Like maybe there's a way to search for the file by name in the drive and get its token. The `list` files API - if I call it with the user's token and filter by filename, I might be able to find the file and retrieve its token that way.","thinkingSignature":"26d08b846ee11af515e37676fae836b6cb77a64ff907bbf1f2ad94b8228b03b8"},{"type":"text","text":"让我换个思路——刚才上传的文件虽然没返回 token，但文件本身可能已经存在云盘里了。我用文件名搜索一下试试看能不能找到并拿到 token：\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_4qn8y1f3zyec_1","name":"feishu_drive_file","arguments":{"action":"list","order_by":"EditedTime","direction":"DESC"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":41318,"output":603,"cacheRead":33312,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":75233,"cost":{"input":0.012395399999999999,"output":0.0007235999999999999,"cacheRead":0.00199872,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.015117719999999998}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774488479152,"responseId":"0613bea1b67befc9f5643e30a528bc0e"}}
