{"type":"message","id":"8a01dd32","parentId":"1c21e06d","timestamp":"2026-03-26T06:01:14.739Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see that `feishu_doc_media.insert` already has the `docs:document.media:upload` scope. But `feishu_doc_media.bitabl_upload` is not listed in the tool-scopes.js.\n\nMore importantly, I need to check the tool-scopes to see if my new `bitable_upload` action has the correct scopes assigned. Since I added `bitable_upload` as a new action under `feishu_doc_media`, I need to add the scopes for it.\n\nBut actually, since `feishu_doc_media` already has `docs:document.media:upload` in its scopes (for the `insert` action), and my `bitable_upload` uses the same API endpoint (`drive.v1.media.uploadAll`), the scope is already correct.\n \nThe issue is that when the user attempts the bitable upload and gets an unauthorized error, it's because my new action isn't registered in the tool-scopes configuration. I need to add the scope mapping for `feishu_doc_media.bitabl_upload` to resolve this.\n\nLet me trace through the actual flow though—the real problem might be in how the tool client gets invoked and which scopes it checks against when making the API call. The tool-scopes.js just defines which scopes each tool/action needs, but the actual permission check happens during token acquisition. If the batch auth already granted `docs:document.media:upload`, then the issue is that the tool registration isn't mapping my new action to that scope, so it's failing the scope check before even trying the API call. The tool invocation should work since the scope is already there, but the error message suggests something else is missing - specifically `bitable:app` permission. I'm wondering if the issue is actually with the `extra` parameter I'm sending in the upload request. Looking at the Feishu docs, for bitable uploads the `extra` should contain a `bitablePerm` object, but I'm not sure if I'm formatting it correctly or if that's even the right approach for this particular upload flow.\n\nLet me verify the actual implementation in the doc-media.js file to see if `bitable_upload` is properly registered as a tool action and whether it's using the right scope configuration.\n\nLooking at the error more carefully, it's saying the user lacks `bitable:app` permission. The issue might be that when calling `drive.v1.media.uploadAll` with `parent_type=bitable_image` and `parent_node=app_token`, Feishu is validating that the user actually has access to that specific bitable app, not just general upload permissions. The `bitable:app` scope controls whether the app itself can be accessed by the user.\n\nLooking at the available scopes for this endpoint, it requires at least one of several options including `bitable:app` for full access, `docs:document.media:upload` for upload-only access, or `drive:drive` for general file access. So I need either `bitable:app` or `docs:document.media:upload` to be authorized.\n\nSince `feishu_doc_media` already has `docs:document.media:upload` listed, that scope should be available through batch auth. The user likely just needs to complete the authorization for it. But there's another possibility worth exploring—instead of using the media upload endpoint, I could create a new record in the bitable with an empty attachment field first, then use the update API to modify that record's attachments.\n\nActually, looking at the batch create documentation more carefully, it seems like the `file_token` for attachments should already be recognized by bitable if it's from a previous upload to the same bitable. So if I use the drive upload endpoint with the correct scope and parent info, the resulting token should work when I pass it to the bitable record creation.\n\nBut the core issue remains - I need `docs:document.media:upload` in the user's token for the media upload to work. Let me step back and think about whether there's a completely different approach that doesn't rely on getting new scopes authorized.\n\nI'm realizing the `feishu_doc_media.insert` action already has the `docs:document.media:upload` scope configured, so if I route my upload through that same action with the right parameters for bitable, it should theoretically work without needing additional authorization.\n\nThe problem is that `feishu_doc_media` requires a `doc_id` parameter pointing to an actual Feishu doc, which I don't have. However, since this is a user-authenticated tool, I should be able to call it with any valid parameters - the doc_id is just metadata for the upload, not an authorization check. So maybe I can use the existing insert action by passing it something like the bitable app ID as a workaround.\n\nLooking at the error response more carefully, it lists several required scopes including `docs:document.media:upload`. Since the user already granted this scope through the batch auth card, the issue might be that the token wasn't properly refreshed or cached. Let me have them complete that authorization first and see if that resolves the \"re-authorization\" error.\n\nI'm also realizing that my `bitable_upload` action needs to be properly registered with the tool-scopes configuration. Let me add that registration to ensure the scope is properly associated with the action.","thinkingSignature":"5b3d8220e40e739e069f441275cb80d6ca21cd8573d6ac59c70d0dc8272c3ada"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_2ux5knvy1pzc_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"grep \"feishu_doc_media\" /Users/edy/.openclaw/extensions/openclaw-lark/src/core/tool-scopes.js"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":67500,"output":1133,"cacheRead":33389,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":102022,"cost":{"input":0.02025,"output":0.0013595999999999999,"cacheRead":0.0020033399999999997,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.02361294}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774504835783,"responseId":"0613fe85fcb02bb9b08a1552ebd20dac"}}
