{"type":"message","id":"c0eb1180","parentId":"74e8f705","timestamp":"2026-03-26T07:24:02.921Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need the actual table_id for that bitable. Let me try to get it from the bitable metadata. But I don't have the `base:app:read` scope available (it's waiting for authorization).\n\nActually, let me try a different approach. What if I look at the problem from a completely different angle?\n\nInstead of trying to upload to bitable storage directly, what if:\n1. I upload the image to the cloud drive using `drive.file.uploadAll` (which works fine)\n2. I then create an image block in a temporary Feishu doc using `doc_media.insert`\n3. The doc image upload uses `drive.media.uploadAll` with `docx_image` parent_type\n\n\n4. The resulting token starts with `boxcn` and has a `url` field that could potentially be used in bitable\n\nThe key insight is that doc images and bitable images might have different storage requirements—the doc token has `drive_route_token` linking it to the doc, while bitable tokens rely on `extra.bitablePerm.tableId` instead. Maybe I can upload the file to cloud drive first, then somehow convert it to a bitable-compatible token, or perhaps pass a download URL directly to bitable instead of a token.\n\nLooking at how Feishu actually handles this in the UI, when you add an attachment it makes an internal API call returning a `file_token` starting with `boxcn`. I'm wondering if there's a dedicated internal endpoint for bitable uploads that the plugin just isn't exposing—maybe the approach should be uploading to cloud drive first, then calling an attachment API to register it with the bitable record. But the standard API flow is clear: `drive.media.uploadAll` to get the token, then `bitable.v1.record.create` or `update` to attach it.\n\nLooking at the Feishu documentation for `bitable_image` and `bitable_file`, the `parent_node` is described as \"The parent_node is used to specify the specific document or location to which the media will be uploaded.\" For doc images it's the doc ID, for sheet images it's the sheet token—so for bitable images it should be the bitable's node token in the drive hierarchy. The issue is that `app_token` and `node_token` are different, and I need to find the correct node token for this bitable. When uploading through the UI, the system automatically handles mapping the parent node properly.\n\nI'm considering whether `drive_route_token` in the `extra` field might be the key—maybe passing the bitable app_token there would work like it does for doc images. But thinking about the actual Feishu architecture, every resource has both an `app_token` (for API calls) and a `node_id` (for the drive hierarchy), and `parent_node` likely needs the `node_id`, not the `app_token`. I could try listing files in the user's drive to find the bitable's node_id, though I don't have explicit folder permissions. Actually, I'm realizing there might be a much simpler solution entirely.","thinkingSignature":"1f13b8a73b25a7d38020b917c6aa31dbadbbf3a72652628a280a859f5f0b5188"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_jf37cyfaub86_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"grep -r \"parent_type.*bitable\\|bitable.*parent\" /Users/edy/.openclaw/extensions/openclaw-lark/node_modules/openclaw/dist/extensions/feishu/node_modules/@larksuiteoapi/node-sdk/lib/index.js 2>/dev/null | grep -i \"bitable\" | head -10"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":82257,"output":722,"cacheRead":33389,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":116368,"cost":{"input":0.0246771,"output":0.0008663999999999999,"cacheRead":0.0020033399999999997,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.02754684}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774509815764,"responseId":"061411fbf7b890dd55415ce55b39348f"}}
