{"type":"message","id":"65c27c8b","parentId":"d2741c80","timestamp":"2026-03-26T08:26:54.442Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Same error. The problem is clearly that `parent_node` with the bitable app_token doesn't work.\n\nLet me think about this completely differently. Maybe the issue is that `drive.media.uploadAll` for `bitable_image` requires the bitable to be accessed through a DIFFERENT token system - maybe the wiki/Drive系统 uses different tokens.\n\nOr maybe... wait. Let me look at the error message again: \"parent node not exist.\" \n\nWhat if this error is NOT about the Drive filesystem, but about the media upload service's understanding of \"parent\"? For `bitable_image`, maybe the `parent_node` needs to be the TABLE node, not the APP node?\n \nFor bitable specifically, the parent_node might need to reference the table itself rather than the app. Looking at that Go SDK example, it seems like `baseToken` is the app-level token and `tableID` is separate, which suggests the upload might need both pieces of information to properly locate the parent.\n\nI'm also noticing the distinction between `drive.file.uploadAll` (which goes to Drive and returns `file_` tokens) and `drive.media.uploadAll` (which returns `boxcn` tokens). The media API seems to be where attachments belong, but the parent_node validation is failing even when I have the right app_token and table_id combination. Maybe the issue isn't the token itself but how the parent_node is being validated against the bitable's actual structure in the system.\n\nLet me reconsider the approach entirely—instead of trying to make the parent_node work, I could upload the image to a regular folder first, then create a record with the attachment pointing to that uploaded file.\n\nBut I've already tested this and it failed because files uploaded to Drive can't be attached to bitable records due to permission boundaries. So that won't work either.\n\nActually, wait. I just realized something about the token formats. Looking at the Drive file listing, the bitable has token `UJZtbmw3Hav0zgs0RPzcvcrPnqe` in the `UJZt` format, but for media uploads the parent_type should be `bitable_image`. The problem might be that bitable tokens in the `UJZt` format aren't actually valid for media upload parent nodes—I might need a `bascn` format token instead, which the example shows as `bascni70rJDWTKvGNOmGHmabcef`.\n\nThis makes me think there's a conversion happening somewhere. When you create a bitable through the API, you get a `UJZt` token, but when created through the UI, it might get a `bascn` token instead. Let me try using the folder's `nodcnKUq3QN9lNcCRr9WtG8pjvf` token as the parent node instead.\n\nActually, wait. The folder token is just a regular folder, not a bitable, so that won't work for `bitable_image` uploads. Looking at the Feishu documentation more carefully, the `parent_node` for bitable_image should be the app_token, but maybe there's a timing issue where newly created bitables don't immediately get a valid Drive node. Though that doesn't make sense since the bitable already appears in the file list.\n\nLet me reconsider the Feishu documentation structure. The upload endpoint uses `/open-apis/drive/v1/medias/upload_all`, but there might be a separate dedicated bitable attachment upload endpoint at `/open-apis/bitable/v1/`. Let me search for that instead of trying to force the media upload to work.\n\nActually, I should look at the bitable field API documentation more carefully—there might be a direct upload action available for attachments that doesn't rely on the parent_node parameter at all.\n\nLet me try the most direct approach: create a record first with the attachment field left empty, then update it with the actual file token. The tricky part is getting that file token in the first place. Looking at the error message more closely—\"parent node not exist\"—this comes from the Drive service and typically means the folder or document token you're trying to use as a parent doesn't actually exist or isn't accessible.\n\nI'm wondering if there's a mismatch between how bitable tokens are represented. The app_token might be one format while the Drive node token is another, and maybe the media upload API needs the Drive node token specifically. When I look at the file list API response, the token shown for a bitable is just `UJZtbmw3Hav0zgs0RPzcvcrPnqe`—which looks like the app_token itself, not a separate node identifier. Let me just attempt the upload with an empty string as the parent_node and see what happens.","thinkingSignature":"aa7cb42a1452e890f7563a935a3a4321a69ec664c85781f0627681c8e6ee0924"},{"type":"text","text":"还是 `parent node not exist.`。\n\n让我彻底换个思路——试试 `parent_node` 用空字符串，看飞书 API 怎么反应：\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_j8z01d5l15dh_1","name":"edit","arguments":{"file_path":"/Users/edy/.openclaw/extensions/openclaw-lark/src/tools/oapi/drive/doc-media.js","old_string":"    const parentType = mediaType === 'image' ? 'bitable_image' : 'bitable_file';\n    log.info(`bitable_upload: app=${p.app_token}, table=${p.table_id}, field=${p.field_id}, file=${fileName}, size=${fileSize}`);\n    // 对于 bitable_image/bitable_file，parent_node 应为 bitable 在 Drive 里的 token（即 app_token）\n    const uploadRes = await client.invoke('feishu_doc_media.bitable_upload', (sdk, opts) => sdk.drive.v1.media.uploadAll({\n        data: {\n            file_name: fileName,\n            parent_type: parentType,\n            parent_node: p.app_token,\n            size: fileSize,\n            file: (0, node_fs_1.createReadStream)(filePath),\n            extra: JSON.stringify({\n                bitablePerm: {\n                    tableId: p.table_id,\n                },\n            }),\n        },\n    }, opts), { as: 'user' });","new_string":"    const parentType = mediaType === 'image' ? 'bitable_image' : 'bitable_file';\n    log.info(`bitable_upload: app=${p.app_token}, table=${p.table_id}, field=${p.field_